1. `push(item: T)`: Adds an item to the top of the stack.
2. `pop(): T | undefined`: Removes and returns the item at the top of the stack. If the stack is empty, returns `undefined`.
3. `peek(): T | undefined`: Returns the item at the top of the stack without removing it. If the stack is empty, returns `undefined`.
4. `isEmpty(): boolean`: Returns `true` if the stack is empty, `false` otherwise.

You should use a TypeScript class to implement the stack and ensure that it is type-safe, meaning it should be able to store and retrieve items of any valid TypeScript type.

Your task is to complete the implementation of the `Stack` class by filling in the missing code for the methods mentioned above.

```typescript
class Stack<T> {
  private items: T[] = [];

  // Adds an item to the top of the stack
  push(item: T): void {
    this.items.push(item);
  }

  // Removes and returns the item at the top of the stack
  pop(): T | undefined {
    return this.items.pop();
  }

  // Returns the item at the top of the stack without removing it
  peek(): T | undefined {
    return this.items[this.items.length - 1];
  }

  // Returns true if the stack is empty, false otherwise
  isEmpty(): boolean {
    return this.items.length === 0;
  }
}

